A||||12||Uaana||Torque Steer?||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||12-11-2001||07:30 PM||||Yes I know I shouldn't have this problem But I can literally feel the car flex and squat on the right rear corner when I goose the gas at speed..<P>Any ideas?<BR>The dog bone could use replacing but I can't see it causing this much of a prob.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||12-11-2001||07:46 PM||||Bad motor/trans mount? Sounds like the engine is twisting in the cradle. The doggie-bone controls lateral movement... I'd check your mounts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000002||87GTZ34||12-11-2001||07:57 PM||||Actually, the engine twisting in th cradle shouldn't cause torque steer but the cradle flexing on the frame certainly will.  You should check them out asap!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||12-11-2001||08:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Actually, the engine twisting in th cradle shouldn't cause torque steer but the cradle flexing on the frame certainly will.  You should check them out asap!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>If the motor is shifting, it will upset the wieght balance. The existing tq-steer will be exagerrated... At least, that's how I see it...<P>The other thing is that you could have a bum spring and or blown-out struts... Check the suspension... If there's any play, expect it to act WEIRD.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000004||shark93726||12-11-2001||08:58 PM||||The most likely cause of torque-steer under throttle is bad rubber bushings in the rear A-arms.<P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadero2dmax||12-11-2001||09:12 PM||||I disagree, on the 84 - 87 chassis, the most likely cause of torque steer is the cradle bushings. You say the right rear You didn't say what year Fiero, but I suspect the early chassis.<P>If it IS an '88, then Gerald is 100% accurate.<P>Another possibility would be a collapsed strut on the corner you feel squatting.<P>Once on an autocross course I had a terrific amount of torque steer - happened suddenly. My car was so low that the swaybar would occasionally drag the ground (this was before I installed the '88 cradle). The bolt head had ground down so much that on this particular corner it pulled out of the swaybar mount, and thus I had only one side of the rear swaybar attached. What a wild ride THAT gave me!!<P>If you have a rear swaybar, check the attach links and the mounting points.<P>My best advice is to check any attach point that is bushed. Start with the 4 points on the cradle if you have an early chassis, then the rear A-arms. Then the strut tower, then the struts themselves. While doing that, look at the spring for grins. If you have an '88, check the links and their bushings. Don't forget to pull a wheel and check the hubs.<P>If you still haven't found a problem, let me know and I will brainstorm some more.<BR>   <P>------------------<BR>Injected 4.9 Cad powered 85 SE (Daily Driver)5 speed ('88 cradle)<P>86 carburated 4.9 automatic Grassroots Motorsports Magazine $2002 Challenge Fiero coupe (2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit) all on an '88 cradle<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<P>George Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||12-11-2001||09:19 PM||||Like George said, check *everything* that is bushed in your suspension.  You will probally have to replace ALL OF IT.  <P>Also, replace all engine mounts, if the dogbone is shot, so are the rest.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>If it IS an '88, then Gerald is 100% accurate.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Except that the 88s don't have A-arms :S) <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||
Z||000007||cadero2dmax||12-11-2001||09:31 PM||||Yeah, I got a little tongue tied. But later on I said links for the '88 - - I got it half right!!<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000008||Uaana||12-11-2001||11:07 PM||||Ok forgot a few things. <BR>1. This is a relativly new set up (3.4 install last July)<BR>2. At that time all the mounts checked out except the doggie.<BR>3. New ST springs and KYB struts.<BR>4. This gets grossly exaggerated when the tire pressure on the right rear goes down a bit Sumitomo's PSI 50-55 normal<BR>5. I have a half axle that has been throwing grease since the install. (loose linkage?)<BR>So could the bad doggie be throwing this off that much? or is it a bad tire?<BR>Or the dying half axle?<P>Or should I add a rear sway bar,  and strut tower brace?<P>BTW it's an 85 SE stripped except for the power mirror's. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||12-12-2001||12:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Uaana:<BR><B>Ok forgot a few things. <BR>1. This is a relativly new set up (3.4 install last July)<BR>2. At that time all the mounts checked out except the doggie.<BR>3. New ST springs and KYB struts.<BR>4. This gets grossly exaggerated when the tire pressure on the right rear goes down a bit Sumitomo's PSI 50-55 normal<BR>5. I have a half axle that has been throwing grease since the install. (loose linkage?)<BR>So could the bad doggie be throwing this off that much? or is it a bad tire?<BR>Or the dying half axle?<P>Or should I add a rear sway bar,  and strut tower brace?<P>BTW it's an 85 SE stripped except for the power mirror's. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are they directional tires? You might have one flipped...<P>||||reg||5
Z||000010||SKIDMARK||12-12-2001||12:50 AM||||How about lower ball joints and tie rod ends?<BR>It's set up just like a front wheel drive with these parts and if the tie rod end or  bushing that mounts it to the cradle is bad it will cause torque steer. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||shark93726||12-12-2001||02:08 AM||||If the car torque steers to the right under throttle and a little to the left under hard braking, that would indicate that the right rear is changing toe worse than the left rear. (under throttle it is moving forward and toeing to the left, which makes the car turn right. braking would do exactly the opposite)<P>The primary causes could be: bad tie rod end, bad lower ball joint, bad a-arm bushings, bad wheel bearing, loose or broken bolts that hold camber on strut, bad bushings or loose ones in right side cradle mounts, or even just bad toe setting on right side. (toed in more than left side)<P>If the car torque steers the other way under throttle, to the left, than look on the left side for all the same problems.<P>Try grabbing the right rear tire and see if you can push/pull it in or out of toe adjustment, might help pinpoint problem area.<P>Good luck, hope this helps ya.<P><P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000012||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||08:12 AM||||The very first, very simple thing I would check is your tire pressure. Make sure it's even on both sides.<P>The reason I offer this is that lately I've had leaks in my right rear tire (got a couple plugs in that tire - can't afford to replace it yet) so I've been pumping that one up higher than normal to buy me more time between tire refills... so this means my left tire has 35 pounds of air in it, while the right (leaky tire) fluctuates between 44 (the max listed on the tire) and 25 (when I finally catch it again in a week or so). (Yes, I realize this is potentially unsafe, and it will be properly corrected as soon as I can afford to do it.)<P>Right after I pump it up to 44 and go, I can feel the back end moving around as I accelerate - the back pulls over to one side as I hit the gas hard. If this is what's happenign to you, check the tire pressure first - you may have a slow leak in one side or the other and a 20-pound pressure difference between them... best of all, it's free.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck... I hope it's a simple fix. Suspension stuff adds up <B>quick</B>.||||reg||1
